Session 3 raw notes: Picked up straight from Calixto's cliffhanger monologue into the fight. The
party grouped up early and ate several rounds of the Guardian's slam attack (AOE, ~10 damage) back
to back — nearly the whole party went down at once. The Wood Elf was the exception: she'd already
broken off from the group before the AOE hit, working out positioning, and stayed up alone. Warla
went down once to the Guardian's opening attack; after that the table locked in hard. Shelob held
Calixto's attention for the whole fight, letting everyone else get free hits on him. Y'droj focused
the Guardian the entire fight but only landed modest damage (~6 total). Warla used Ismara as cover
to get Hidden, then used a stealth-crit ebony-arrow technique for ~30 damage a shot — expensive gear
(a pack of 20 ebony arrows runs 2000+ gold) but effective, and it's an allowed, established technique
going forward since the table is playing Legendary Difficulty. The real fight-winner: the Wood Elf
reanimated Calixto's corpse after he died and used it to force-cast Paralyzing Bolt on the Guardian
every round, locking its slam attack and denying it any tactical options. No formal Phase 2 trigger
— instead, after Calixto died, the GM narrated the smoke clearing from the Guardian's eyes and it
getting more aggressive with newer moves as an improvised second phase. Shelob had a mango at some
point that was, per the table, satisfying no matter what had just happened. After the fight: surfaced,
lied to the city about what actually happened. Told the priest they'd succeeded — he was thankful,
then two more Pale Fever victims came in right after, and he said, essentially, "well, at least these
should be the last, if it's really stopped" — which of course it isn't. Ismara had already left the
group earlier and wasn't present for this. Separately, she found Warla, asked for 50 gold, then
revealed — winking, communicated through their bond rather than spoken — that she's "really good at
illusion magic," and shimmered into the appearance of a tall, living Nord woman, implying this is how
she's moved through the city unnoticed the whole time. Then to the Jarl for payment: 800 gold each
(not the flat 800 the job was originally quoted at), plus a free night at the inn. Session ended there.


ISMARA'S DOOM
A Chronicle of the Parks Group

CHAPTER THREE: THE FACE SHE WEARS

PART I: THE LAST ARGUMENT CALIXTO EVER WON
He was still talking when the first blow landed.
"—one more subject. Everything I've done, it's been for this—" and then Shelob was moving, and
the sentence never finished, because nobody in that chamber was listening anymore. The friendly
collector, the man who'd called Warla's markings fascinating and troubling in the same breath
back when this had all still looked like an investigation, had become something with no further
use for an audience. The Guardian's blue-fire eyes tracked him from behind, patient the way ice
is patient.
The party didn't wait to find out which of them he was more afraid of.

PART II: THE FLOOR COMES OUT FROM UNDER THEM
[Wood Elf Name] had already stepped back from the group before the first exchange — reading the
chamber, the braziers, the distance between pillars, working out where the fight would actually
happen instead of where it was starting. It was the reason she was the only one standing thirty
seconds later.
The Guardian's greatsword came around in a wide, unhurried arc, and where it landed, the ice
followed — a wave of cold that didn't care how many of them were bunched together at the mouth
of the chamber, because all of them were. It hit like a wall falls, one motion, nothing personal
about it. Y'droj went down. Shelob went down. Warla, closest to the front, took the worst of it
and didn't get back up.
For one very long second, the only person still on their feet in that chamber was standing thirty
feet away, alone, having already moved.
Then everyone remembered why they were there.

PART III: WHAT SHELOB IS ACTUALLY FOR
Shelob got up first, or got up loudest, which amounted to the same thing. She put herself between
Calixto and everyone else with the specific, total confidence of someone who had already decided
this was her job for the evening and saw no reason to renegotiate. Calixto's attention — his
Paralyzing Bolts, his Drain Life, the whole nervous architecture of a man fighting from as far
away as possible — never left her for the rest of the fight.
Which meant everyone else got to work.

PART IV: THE ARCHER FINDS HER ROOM
Warla came back up angry, in the specific way of someone who has just learned something about
themselves the hard way. She didn't charge back into the open. She found Ismara instead — not
for protection exactly, or not only that — and in the narrow cover the Draugr's stillness
provided, went still herself.
The bow came out. Ebony arrows, the kind that cost more than the job itself was paying. Hidden,
and then not hidden at all, in the way that only matters for the half-second before the string
releases: one arrow, thirty pounds of damage where a good hit should have been ten. Then another.
Whatever the eye did to make that work, it worked the same way twice, and then a third time. She
did not go down again.
Y'droj kept working the Guardian the entire fight, steady and unspectacular — the kind of damage
that adds up slowly and doesn't get talked about afterward, except that it was there the whole
time, wearing the thing down between everyone else's better nights.

PART V: THE DEAD MAN'S LAST USEFUL THOUGHT
Calixto went down under Shelob's attention and everyone else's leftover arrows, and for a moment
the fight looked like it had one villain left instead of two.
[Wood Elf Name] looked at his body on the stone floor of a chamber built by people who'd
understood exactly this kind of magic four thousand years before he was born, and had an idea
that was either brilliant or in extremely poor taste, and did not particularly care which.
She raised him.
Not for revenge, and not for spectacle — for the amulet's last trick. Calixto's own hands, still
warm, still remembering the somatic pattern of Paralyzing Bolt, cast it on the Guardian. Then
again. Then again, every round, a dead man's magic locking the joints of the machine he'd
supposedly controlled — the Guardian's slam arrested mid-swing, its options narrowing to nothing
it could actually use, while the thing that had built it out of fear stood there and did the
one useful thing it had left to give.

PART VI: WHAT WAS UNDER THE SMOKE
The Guardian noticed. Whatever ancient recognition had kept it obedient — Calixto's amulet, the
memory of a command it no longer had a commander for — went out of it the moment its own dead
handler's hands turned against it. Something cleared behind its eyes, the way smoke clears off
a fire that's finally caught properly. It stopped fighting like a thing on a leash.
It got worse. New weight behind the swings, new speed it hadn't shown all fight, moves that
hadn't been part of whatever cautious pattern the amulet had kept it inside. For a few rounds it
was, for the first time all night, actually trying to kill them specifically, all of it, at once.
Warla's arrows didn't stop coming. Y'droj's slow, patient damage didn't stop landing. Shelob held
her ground she'd claimed at the start of the fight and never surrendered it. Calixto's paralyzed
hands kept doing their one job.
It went down mid-swing, the black iron greatsword still rising when the legs underneath it
finally stopped answering. The blue fire in its eyes didn't so much go out as run out — the way
a hearth doesn't die, it just eventually finishes.

PART VII: THE MANGO
Somewhere in the quiet after — the specific, ringing quiet of a fight that is actually finished
and not merely paused — Shelob produced a mango. Where from was not a question anyone asked
anymore; it had stopped being interesting the third time she'd conjured food out of nowhere with
no explanation offered and none wanted. She ate it standing over the wreckage of the fight, with
the same total, uncomplicated satisfaction she brought to everything else, and for a moment that
was the only normal thing in the room.

PART VIII: WHAT THEY TOLD THE CITY
They surfaced with the sky already going gray, told the story they'd agreed on somewhere between
the last body and the stairs up, and the city believed them, because the city wanted to.
The priest was grateful. He said so plainly, the kind of gratitude that comes from someone who
has been carrying bad news for days and finally gets to set it down. That lasted until the doors
opened again and two more people were carried in — same marks, same blue-white light climbing
under the skin from somewhere inside it.
He looked at the two new victims for a long moment, then back at them.
"Well." He didn't finish the thought right away. "You cleared what was down there — I saw the
report myself." A pause. "So whatever this is, it isn't finished."
Nobody in the room had the piece of information that would have explained why.

PART IX: THE FACE SHE WEARS
Ismara was not there for that. She had gone her own way sometime before the two new victims came
through the door, the way she always did when she judged there was nothing left for her to learn
from watching.
She found Warla later, on the way out of the temple grounds. Held up a hand — fifty gold, plain
as that, no explanation offered and, as always, none coming unless asked for directly. Warla
paid it.
Then, as Ismara turned to go, she looked back. Something moved through the bond between them,
dry and almost warm, the closest thing to a smile Warla had learned to read in a woman who
couldn't produce one with her own face anymore.
*I'm quite good at illusion magic, actually. Why do you think no one's reacted to me?*
The Draugr's desiccated form didn't so much change as reconsider itself — the wrappings, the
blue-burning eyes, the whole unmistakable wrongness of her, folding into something the eye
simply agreed to see instead: a tall, living Nord woman, unremarkable, the kind of face a guard
waves through a gate without a second look.
She held the shape for exactly as long as it took to make the point. Then she was gone, in
whatever direction she went when nobody needed to watch her leave.

PART X: THE PRICE OF BEING BELIEVED
The Jarl received them that evening, grateful in the specific, transactional way of someone whose
city has a problem it can now stop actively worrying about. Eight hundred gold, to each of them —
more than the job had been quoted at, and nobody in the room chose to point that out. A free
night at the inn, on the city's coin, before they took the road again.
They took the gold. They took the room. Nobody mentioned Calixto's last entry, the one about
someone else already doing this work, someone who understood the notation the way he never had.
That thread was still sitting exactly where they'd left it — unpulled, and waiting.
